  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV8">
      <l>veṟpaṉ kuṟavar viṭalai yeyiṉa</l>
<l>roṟkamil kuṟumpoṟai nāṭaṉ kuṭavar</l>
<l>makuṇa ṉuḻavar cērppaṉ paratava</l>
<l>riṉaiyaṉa piṟavu makkaṭ peyarē.<anchor xml:id="peyare"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l>The man from the mountain, the hill people, the warrior, 
the hunting tribe,</l> 
<l>the man from a land of small hillocks devoid of destitution, the herdsmen,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṭavar</hi></term> is the only element in this enumeration that is not found in the IAC. It is, however, present in the Caṅkam corpus (see AN 393.16).</note></l> 
<l>the pleasure-man,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">makuṇaṉ</hi></term> may be a dialectal variant of 
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">makiḻnaṉ/makiṇaṉ</hi></term> (both are recorded in the Tamil Lexicon). 
Cf. TNV 3 where we find 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuruñci</hi></term> instead of the more standard 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term>.</note> 
the ploughmen, the man from the coast, the fishermen,</l>
<l>and others of this kind are the names of the people.</l></lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV8comm1">eṉpatu makkaṭ peyar ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.
      </p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the names of the people occur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV8comm2">veṟpaṉ viṭalai kuṟum poṟai nāṭaṉ makuṇaṉ cērppaṉ eṉpaṉa talaimakaṉ peyar. 
      kuṟavar eyiṉar kuṭavar uḻavar paratavar eṉpaṉa van nilattu vāḻvār peyar eṉṟa vāṟu.
      </p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The man from the mountain, the warrior, the man from the land of small
        hillocks, the pleasure-man, and the man from the coast – these are said to be the names of
        the hero. The hill people, the hunting tribe,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The TL theorises <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eyiṉar</hi></q> is a participial noun form from the verb <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eytal</hi></term> (‘to discharge arrows’). This may well be the case, but it is clearly used as a designation for a specific group of people and so it is treated as lexicalised.</note> the herdsmen,
        the ploughmen, and the fishermen – these are said to be the names of the inhabitants of those
        lands.</p></div>
        <div type="commentary" rend="parallel"> 
      <p xml:id="TNV8comm3">piṟavum eṉṟataṉāṟ poruppaṉ malaiyaṉ cilampaṉ koṭicci kuṟatti kuṟavar 
      iṟavuḷar<anchor xml:id="iravular"/> eṉavuṅ kāḷai viṭalai yeyiṉar eyiṟṟi maṟavar<anchor xml:id="maravar"/> eṉavum aṇṇal mīḷi<anchor xml:id="mili"/> yiṭaicciyar āycciyar 
      kōvalar iṭaiyar potuvar āyar<anchor xml:id="ayar"/> eṉavum ūraṉ kiḻatti maṉaivi<anchor xml:id="manaivi"/> kaṭaiyar kaṭaiciyar<anchor xml:id="kataiciyar"/> eṉavuṅ koṇkaṉ 
      ṟuṟaivaṉ ṟuṟaivi paratti nuḷaiyar nuḷaicciyar<anchor xml:id="nulaicciyar"/> eṉavum varuvaṉa vellāṅ koḷka.
      </p>
      <p xml:lang="en">By <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">piṟavum</hi></q>, understand that 
      all [of these] are included: the man from the hill, the man from the mountain, the man from the mountainside, the creeper-woman,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">This is a literal 
      translation that is chosen to retain as much flavour of the original Tamil as possible. In practice, this 
      refers to a slender woman who inhabits the hills of the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term> 
      setting.</note> the woman from the hills, the hill people, and the hill tribe; the young man, the warrior, the 
      hunting tribe, the woman of the hunting tribe, and the bold ones;<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">It is not entirely clear what such terms mean. 
      In practice <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">maṟavar</hi></q> refers to an enemy of a strong warrior, but 
      this is the case for quite a few such designations. When they appear, they are translated quite 
      literally to keep intact as much of the Tamil etymology as possible.</note> the majestic [lord of the 
      forest setting], the commander, the female herders of the middle [region],<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">The 
      ‘middle’ (<foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">iṭai</foreign>) here can be understood as referring to the tract of land in-between the hills 
      (<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term>) and plains (<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutam</hi></term>), 
      so the forest pastures (<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mullai</hi></term>).</note> the female cowherds, 
      the cowherds, the herdsmen of the middle [region],<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">The male herdsmen, presumably.</note> 
      the goatherds,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">potuvar</hi></q> presumably refers to a different class of herders, 
      perhaps of goats rather than cows or other types of cattle. It also appears in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Kalittokai</hi></term>.</note> and 
      the cowherds;<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">āyar</hi></q> might be a slightly later designation referring to the same 
      herders as <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kōvalar</hi></q>, found earlier in this enumeration. <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">āyar</hi></q> 
      only appears once in the ‘old’ corpus in PN 390.1, whereas <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kōvalar</hi></q> is 
      quite common (see KT 358.6, NA 69.8 and AN 155.8 for example).</note> the man from the village, the heroine,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">One 
      could translate this more literally, as the ‘proprietress’ (as done in the TL) – the feminine singular 
      pronominalised noun from <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kiḻamai</hi></term> (‘claim’, ‘right’, ‘property’). 
      However, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kiḻatti</hi></q> is used so widely in both poetry and commentarial 
      literature to refer specifically to the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">talaivi</hi></term> that one can feel 
      confident the referent is the heroine, and that to translate it differently would in this case take us 
      further away from the intended meaning of the word.</note> the housewife,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">maṉaivi</hi></q> also refers to the heroine in her post-marriage life.</note> the men from the peripheries, and the women from the peripheries;<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṭaiyar</hi></q> and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṭaicciyar</hi></q> are not designations found in the Caṅkam corpus. It is thought that they refer to a group of people with low societal status (see the entry in the TL). ‘Periphery’ is chosen here, as it most closely mirrors the Tamil where
       <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṭai</hi></q> means ‘boundary, limit’. This designation may be making a purely spatial observation that people existed on the peripheries of either a 
       settlement or a concept of society – a spatial fact that probably reflected social status. Otherwise, this may refer to peripheral characters – the intermediaries for the quarrelling couple.</note> the 
      man from the shore, the man from the ghat, the woman from the ghat, the maritime woman, the men of the fishermen caste, 
      and the women of the fishermen caste.</p>
      </div>
      </body>
      </text>
